.SH NAME
popen, pclose \- initiate I/O to or from a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fB#include <stdio.h>
.PP
\fBFILE *popen\fI(command, type)
\fBchar \fI*command, *type;
.PP
\fBint pclose\fI(stream)
\fBFILE \fI*stream;\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The arguments to 
.B popen
are pointers to null-terminated strings
containing respectively a shell command line and an I/O
mode, either \fIr\fR for reading or \fIw\fR for writing.
It creates a pipe between
the calling process and
the command to be executed.
The value returned is a stream 
pointer that can write to the standard input
of the command or read from its standard output.
.PP
A stream opened by
.B popen
should be closed by
.B pclose,
which waits for the associated process to terminate
and returns the exit status of the command.
.PP
Because open files are shared, a type \fIr\fR command
is used as an input filter, and a type \fIw\fR as an output filter.
.SH SEE ALSO
pipe(2),
fopen(3),
fclose(3),
system(3),
wait(2).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Popen
returns a null pointer
if files or processes cannot be created, or the shell 
cannot be accessed.
.PP
.B Pclose
returns \-1 if
.I stream
is not associated with a \fIpopened\fR command.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Buffered reading before opening an input filter
sometimes leaves the standard input of that filter mispositioned.
Similar problems with an output filter 
forestalled by careful buffer flushing, for example, with
.I fflush
(
.BR fclose (3)).
